  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nAnother seasonably warm day as temperatures warm well into the \n50s this afternoon under clear skies. More widespread cloud \ncover across northern Minnesota has nosed southwards into the \nAlexandria/St. Cloud/Mille Lacs region of central Minnesota & \nwill keep temperatures a few degrees cooler. Even warmer \ntemperatures are expected tomorrow as southerly warm advection \nramps up again overnight in response to a deepening low \npressure system tracking from the northern Plains into Ontario.\nHighs in the low 60s are expected across much of the area, & \ncould be a few degrees higher deepening on the amount of low & \nmid-level cloud cover. We could end up breaking a 100+ year old\nrecord with MSP's record high being 63 degrees set back in \n1925. Fueling the record warm temperatures will be gusty \nsoutherly winds in response to the tightening pressure gradient\nover the area, with gusts of 30-40 mph expected area-wide from \nlate morning through early afternoon. Stronger gusts are likely\nacross southern Minnesota, but any chance for gusts near 50 mph\nlooks to be constrained to far-southwest Minnesota.\n\nRain chances increase Friday night as the continued warm & moist\nadvection develops shallow lift for some light rain or drizzle\nthrough the night. Uncertain just how much precipitation we'll\nsee from this forcing, with AI models depicting around 0.1\" of\nlight rain/drizzle through the night across the area while\nphysical models keep things relatively dry. Drizzle chances \nwill be highest closer to the surface low highest central \nMinnesota & northwest Wisconsin. Any appreciable rainfall will \nhold off until later Saturday morning & afternoon as a narrow \nband of showers develops along the surface cold front. \nConsensus model timing has the front through the I-35 corridor \nby the time precipitation really begins to develop along the \nfront. so the threat for rain during the day Saturday looks to \nmainly be limited to far-eastern/southeastern Minnesota & \nwestern Wisconsin. The heaviest rain may end up falling even \nfarther east across eastern Iowa & central Minnesota, but \ngenerally expected amounts of 0.5-1\" along the main band of \nrain.\n\nTemperatures will begin falling through the afternoon behind \nthe frontal passage, but we won't see a sharp drop in \ntemperatures as is common in winter-time cold fronts. Instead, \nwe'll see an extended stretch of falling temperatures as cold \nair advection continues under strong Canadian high pressure. \nSunday's highs will be in the 40s at midnight & be falling \nthrough the day, with afternoon temperatures in the mid 30s to \nupper 30s. the real cold air arrives Monday & Tuesday as a near \n1040 mb Canadian high settles over the region. Afternoon highs \nwill struggle to warm out of the 20s along with low \ntemperatures well into the teens. Temperatures begin to warm \nthrough the rest of the week as southerly flow returns on the \nback side of the high, but we'll be solidly in a more early \nwinter-like regime as highs stay in the 30s & lows in the 20s \nthrough the rest of November. Little in the way of precipitation\nis expected through the Thanksgiving holiday with dry high \npressure dominating our weather pattern through next week.",
